<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fotudeng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 348</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Fotucheng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fotudeng was a Buddhist monk from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, probably Kucha, who arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 310. He became adviser to the Later Zhao rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-le">Shi Le</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-hu">Shi Hu</a>, gaining their favour through reputed miracles and prophecies. With their support he founded hundreds of temples and trained many disciples, including Dao'an, helping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> take root in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fotudeng.jpg" alt="Fotudeng" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous ancient wood block print illustration. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddhacinga_Fotudeng.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
